---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EditorialNote from '../components/EditorialNote.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';
const a = site.analytics;
const analyticsOn = a.approvedByArtist && a.domain;
---
<BaseLayout title="privacy" description="How GilesMcCrary.com handles contact details and visitor data.">
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <span class="label">privacy</span>
    <h1>privacy</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap prose">
    <EditorialNote>
      Please read and confirm this page before launch, particularly the retention period.
      It reflects how the site is built; it is not legal advice.
    </EditorialNote>

    <h2>contact form</h2>
    <p>
      When you use the contact form, the site collects your name, your email address, an
      optional subject, and your message. These are used only to read and reply to your
      message. They are not added to a mailing list, sold, or shared with anyone else.
    </p>
    <p>
      Form submissions are received and stored by the site’s host, Netlify, which also
      screens them for spam. As part of that screening, technical details such as your IP
      address and browser type may be processed. Messages are kept only as long as needed
      to respond, then deleted.
    </p>

    <h2>the field</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>What is stored with a piece.</strong> If you choose to leave a piece in the
      field, the site stores its letters, their arrangement, the date, the version of the
      <a href="/field/terms/">participation terms</a> you agreed to, and its moderation state
      (for example, whether it has been reported). The word you typed is checked automatically
      and then discarded; it is not stored separately. No name, email address, IP address,
      or device identifier is stored with your piece.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What the host processes.</strong> Like every website, the site is delivered by
      its host, Netlify, which necessarily processes your IP address and basic request
      details (such as browser type and the address requested) to serve pages, protect the
      site, and enforce rate limits on submissions. Netlify may keep this information in its
      own logs for a limited time under its own privacy policy. The site’s code does not
      record it, and it is never linked to your piece.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Your removal key.</strong> When you leave a piece, the server creates a random
      key, gives it once to your browser, and keeps only a one-way fingerprint (a hash) of it.
      Your browser saves the key in its local storage (not a cookie). With it, and only with
      it, you can remove your piece from its link on the same device. If you clear your
      browser data, the key is gone; you can still ask for removal through the
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>, quoting the piece’s link.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>When a piece is removed</strong> (by you or in moderation), its letters are
      erased from the live record at once and it disappears from the field. A small empty
      record remains so that old links show it is gone. The host may keep operational copies
      of stored data for a limited period under its own practices; these are not used to show
      the piece again. Images or links that others have already shared cannot be recalled.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing you do in <a href="/abstract/">abstract your own concrete</a> leaves your
      browser unless you choose to leave it in the field.
    </p>

    <h2>analytics</h2>
    {analyticsOn ? (
      <p>
        The site uses Plausible Analytics to count visits in aggregate (pages viewed,
        referring sites, country, device type). It sets no cookies and collects no
        personal information. No visitor is identified or tracked across other websites.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        The site does not currently use analytics, advertising, or tracking cookies.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2>cookies</h2>
    <p>
      The public site sets no cookies. Your browser’s local storage is used only for the
      field’s removal keys (above) and to remember whether you hid the exhibition guide.
    </p>

    <h2>server logs</h2>
    <p>
      As with any website, the host processes and briefly logs technical information such as
      IP addresses and requested pages in order to deliver the site securely and reliably.
    </p>

    <h2>your choices</h2>
    <p>
      To ask what information is held about you, or to have a message you sent deleted,
      please get in touch through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>{site.contactEmail && <> or at <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a></>}.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
